.TH SAY 1
.SH NAME
say \- speak, through ml(3)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B say
[
.B -v
.I voice
]
[
.B -u
.I units
]
[
.B -l
.I lexicon
]
[
.B -o
.I file
]
[
.B -i
]
.B -t
.I text
|
.B -p
.I phonemes
|
.I id ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Say
runs a voice model, through
.IR voice (2),
and writes the result to
.B /dev/audio
\(em or to
.I file
with
.BR -o ,
as signed 16-bit little-endian mono.
.B -i
first prints what the model declares.
.B -n
prints the sounds and speaks nothing, which is how a tutorial's narration is
checked without loading sixty megabytes of model.
.PP
.B -t
takes English.
Each word is looked up in a lexicon by
.IR phon (2)
\(em
.B -l
names a different one \(em and a word that is not there stops
.B say
and names the word, rather than being guessed at.
That front end is deliberately outside the synthesizer: turning spelling into
sounds is a separate job with its own failure mode, and a wrong guess is silent
where a refusal is not.
.PP
Either
.B -t
or
.B -p
can be given more than once, in any order relative to each other.
Each repeat is another utterance, spoken in the order given, through one model
load \(em not one load per utterance, which is what made a script with several
lines to say cost seconds it did not need to.
Flags must come before any bare
.I id...
arguments; this parser stops looking for flags at the first one it does not
recognise.
.PP
.B -u
asks
.IR ml (3)
for particular compute units.
The default is
.BR cpu ,
and deliberately: above about forty phonemes
.IR onnxruntime 's
CoreML provider faults inside Apple's
.SM BNNS
convolution and takes the model server with it.
That was measured outside Inferno entirely \(em the same five utterances
through a fifteen-line driver, three of five dead on CoreML and five of five
good on the processor \(em so it is not this system's bug to fix.
.B "-u all"
asks for the accelerator anyway, and short utterances do work there.
.PP
The voice is a VITS model: phoneme numbers in, a waveform out, with no vocoder
or formant synthesis in between.
That is the reason for putting it behind
.IR ml (3)
rather than building a synthesizer \(em the interesting part is a trained
network, and the device already runs those.
.SH "IT TAKES PHONEMES, NOT TEXT"
Turning text into phonemes is a different job with a different failure mode,
which is English spelling, and doing it badly here would make a working
synthesizer look broken.
A front end belongs on top of this, not inside it.
.PP
The ids are the voice's own, from the
.B phoneme_id_map
in its
.B .onnx.json
file.
This model wants them interleaved with the pad symbol and wrapped in begin and
end:
.IP
.EX
say 1 0 20 0 59 0 24 0 120 0 27 0 100 0 2
say: 11008 samples, .688 seconds at 16000 Hz
.EE
.PP
which is
.BR hello .
.SH "IT NEEDS A BIGGER POOL THAN THE DEFAULT"
A voice is tens of megabytes and
.IR emu (1)'s
main pool is 32MB, so the model does not fit and the write fails with
.BR "out of memory: kernel" .
.IP
.EX
emu -pmain=512m -pheap=512m ...
.EE
.SH "WHAT WAS CHECKED"
Not by listening, which cannot be automated and does not regress.
The output of
.B hello
is 0.69 seconds, which is right for the word; its peak is 20187 of 32767, so it
is neither silent nor clipped; six of its seventeen frames are near-silent,
which is the lead-in and lead-out of a short utterance; its envelope varies by
a factor of five thousand within the utterance, where noise would be flat; and
it crosses zero 3033 times a second, which is the voiced-speech range rather
than a tone or a hiss.
.PP
Saying the word twice gives 0.94 seconds rather than 0.69 \(em longer, and not
double, because the model's duration predictor treats the phrase as a unit.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/say.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/voice.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR voice (2),
.IR speakertest (1),
.IR phon (2),
.IR ml (3),
.IR audio (3)
.SH BUGS
Flags must come before any bare
.I id...
arguments \(em see DESCRIPTION.
A flag placed after one is silently swallowed as more ids rather than
rejected: the parser stops recognising flags at the first argument it does
not know what to do with, and Limbo's
.B int
of something that is not a number is 0 rather than an error, so it does not
even fail loudly.
.PP
.B -o
to a file, given more than one
.B -t
or
.BR -p ,
keeps only the last utterance: each write to a named file starts it over,
where
.B /dev/audio
does not.
See
.IR voice (2)'s
own BUGS for why \(em it is
.BR play 's
behaviour, not something this front end adds to it.
.PP
One voice, named in the source as a default.
.PP
It holds the whole waveform before writing any of it, so a long utterance is a
long silence and then all of it at once.
A model that can be run in pieces would stream; this one is asked for a phrase
and answers with a phrase.
.PP
Nothing checks that the ids belong to the voice.
An id past the end of its table is the voice's problem, and what it does about
it is not defined here.
.PP
The scales \(em how noisy, how fast, how variable \(em are fixed at what the
voice's own configuration recommends.
